      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright© 2017, International Institute of Anticancer Research (Dr. George J. Delinasios), All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "Label": "BACKGROUND/AIM",
            "content": "There is no clear consensus on the type of adjuvant therapy that should be used for patients with extrahepatic bile duct cancer."
          },
          {
            "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
            "content": "Two hundred and seventy-one patients that had undergone surgical resection for extrahepatic bile duct cancer composed the study cohort. Demographics, treatments, and relationships between the potential prognostic factors and survival rates were analyzed."
          },
          {
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The overall 3-year and 5-year survival rates for post-surgery extrahepatic bile duct cancer patients were 49.0% and 35.4%, respectively. Multivariate analysis revealed that regional lymph node metastasis was an independent negative prognostic factor. We observed a significant correlation between node-positive extrahepatic bile duct cancer and postoperative local recurrence, liver metastasis, peritoneal dissemination, and post-surgery lymph node metastasis. Adjuvant S-1 chemotherapy showed a favorable hazard ratio in patients with lymph node metastases or positive vascular invasion."
          },
          {
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "We recommend the use of adjuvant S-1 therapy in patients with lymph node metastases or microvascular invasion."
          }
        ]
      },
